#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345151 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152157 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c3bf9ea3-d57f-53f6-aa3c-eddffe1a8cfa" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Given Set Is a Basis of the Column Space of a Matrix For a Larger Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3214ac66-089e-5f1a-b5f5-27127d89af81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342662} {:answer/id #uuid "5e6f8a38-0a5a-5b8d-9bbd-8d6b55a7767b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343032} {:answer/id #uuid "69df0ecf-34a7-5ab2-a85a-47fd55e5f70c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343142} {:answer/id #uuid "fd9fef81-c774-530d-bce3-3ec15ebb79e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344469} {:answer/id #uuid "f635b87e-9ad6-595e-8ac8-cf7446521d1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{3}\\}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(V)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423617}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f635b87e-9ad6-595e-8ac8-cf7446521d1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{3}\\}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(V)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423617} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab78a2e2-73c6-516c-bb41-33e676435ed4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344794}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c466a2ff-d594-54f8-8655-5aeb347fd10d" :question/math-academy-id "q-75980" :question/problem "The matrix $V$ is given below. Let ${v}_{1}$, ${v}_{2}$,  ${v}_{3}$ and ${v}_{4}$ be the respective columns of $V.$\nDetermine if $B=\\text{{}{v}_{1},{v}_{3}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(V).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}$, where ${v}_{2}={x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}.$ \n\n$$\nV=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 10 & 20 & 10 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $V$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 10 & 20 & 10 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=(-\\frac{1}{3}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 10 & 20 & 10 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 10 & 20 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{10}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st, $2$nd, and $4$th columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(V)$ is $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{4}\\}.$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{3}\\}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(V).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423621 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152157 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c3bf9ea3-d57f-53f6-aa3c-eddffe1a8cfa" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Given Set Is a Basis of the Column Space of a Matrix For a Larger Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35e3091b-8980-52e7-a1d9-a90be4aa96ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\{{f}_{1},{f}_{2},{f}_{3}\\}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(F)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423604} {:answer/id #uuid "3d86af19-6bb9-5da7-832b-79a566c84f6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423605} {:answer/id #uuid "67c52e20-2bf3-5d92-86ef-d45fa30e8513" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423606} {:answer/id #uuid "9f2e8bb6-fedc-5092-b791-68f16dc74270" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423611} {:answer/id #uuid "a9845672-a026-53af-b2ba-1e8d7af5f91b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423612}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35e3091b-8980-52e7-a1d9-a90be4aa96ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\{{f}_{1},{f}_{2},{f}_{3}\\}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(F)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423604} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7f4cbcc-1506-5396-b835-9d7d9a6a3ab8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423619}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "693e6ed3-909b-52a6-8fa3-e0e6ae013327" :question/math-academy-id "q-75982" :question/problem "The matrix $F$ is given below. Let ${f}_{1}$, ${f}_{2}$,  ${f}_{3}$ and ${f}_{4}$ be the respective columns of $F.$\nDetermine if $B=\\text{{}{f}_{1},{f}_{2},{f}_{3}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(F).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}$, where ${f}_{4}={x}_{1}{f}_{1}+{x}_{2}{f}_{2}+{x}_{3}{f}_{3}.$ \n\n$$\nF=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -4 & -24 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -6 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $F$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -4 & -24 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -6 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=(-\\frac{1}{4}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 6 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -6 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 6 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(F)$ is $\\{{f}_{1},{f}_{2}\\}.$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that $B=\\{{f}_{1},{f}_{2},{f}_{3}\\}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(F).$"}]}
